weeks = [
 dict(w=21,focus='在规定时间内直接回应；检查题卡覆盖，选择并回应追问',
 p1=['Do you prefer making plans on paper or on a screen?','When do you usually check your plans?','How do you remember small tasks?','Has the way you organise your time changed?'],
 p1follow=['What happens when a plan changes unexpectedly?','Would the same method suit everyone?'],
 short="I normally use a paper notebook for personal plans because I can see the whole week at once. For appointments that may change, though, I use my phone. It can remind me before I leave, so I do not have to keep checking the notebook while I am travelling.",
 card='Describe a time you checked something carefully before using it.\n\nSay:\n- what you checked\n- why you checked it\n- how you checked it\n- and explain whether the check was useful.',
 round=['Would you do the same check again?','Who else might find that check useful?'],
 long="""I would like to talk about checking the route for a walk I was planning with a friend. We wanted to visit a lake outside our town, and I had found a route description online.

Before we left, I noticed that the description had been written several years earlier. I was not sure whether the small footbridge mentioned in it was still open, so I compared it with a more recent notice from the park. That notice said the bridge was closed for repairs. I then marked a different path on a paper map and saved a copy on my phone.

The check took only a few minutes, but it changed where we started the walk. We also allowed more time because the alternative route was longer. When we reached the park, there was a barrier beside the old bridge, exactly as the notice had explained.

I found the check useful because it turned a possible interruption into an ordinary part of our plan. It did not remove every uncertainty, such as the weather, but it dealt with one specific question before it caused a problem.""",
 fullp1=['What kind of place do you study or work in?','Do you like a quiet environment?','What do you keep on your desk?','How do you choose a place to sit?','Do you often change your surroundings?','What helps you concentrate?'],
 fullcard='Describe a useful instruction you followed.\n\nSay:\n- what it was for\n- where you found it\n- what you did\n- and explain what made it useful.',
 fullround=['Was any part difficult to follow?','Would you recommend that instruction to someone else?'],
 fulllong="""One useful instruction I followed explained how to assemble a small reading lamp. I bought it for a desk at home, and the parts came with a single sheet of diagrams.

At first I thought I could work out the assembly by looking at the finished picture. However, two of the screws looked almost identical, so I decided to follow the numbered steps. The sheet showed each screw beside its measurement. I put the parts into separate groups and checked the first diagram before attaching the base.

The most helpful detail was a close view of the joint between the arm and the shade. It showed which side a small washer belonged on. Without that picture, I might have placed it on the opposite side and had to take the joint apart again. Once the lamp was assembled, I checked that the base was stable before using it.

I liked the instruction because each picture answered a question at the moment I needed it. There was very little text, but the order and the labels were clear. For this particular task, that was more useful than a long general explanation.""",
 p3=['What makes an instruction clear?','Why do people sometimes ignore instructions?','Should public instructions include pictures?','How can organisations check whether their instructions work?'],
 deepfollow=['Could too much detail create difficulties?','When might written instructions be insufficient?'],
 deep="Clear instructions should tell people what to do next and make important conditions easy to find. A numbered sequence helps with a practical task, while a diagram can explain a position more quickly than a paragraph. However, extra detail is not always helpful. If every possible exception appears before the first step, readers may lose track of the main action. I would test the instructions with someone unfamiliar with the task and watch where they hesitate."),
 dict(w=22,focus='原创阶段检查与成绩记录分开；解释判断依据和局限',
 p1=['Do you enjoy learning practical skills?','How do you choose something new to learn?','Do you prefer demonstrations or written explanations?','How do you notice that you are improving?'],
 p1follow=['Does quick progress always mean good learning?','What might make you change your learning method?'],
 short="I usually notice improvement when I can do a task without looking at every instruction. For example, with a new recipe, I may remember the order after trying it a few times. That does not mean the result is perfect, but it shows that one part of the process has become easier.",
 card='Describe something you learned by observing another person.\n\nSay:\n- what you learned\n- whom you observed\n- what you noticed\n- and explain how the observation helped you.',
 round=['Would a written explanation have helped as well?','Would you like to develop that skill further?'],
 long="""I learned a simple way of wrapping a fragile object by watching a friend pack a small ceramic bowl. We were preparing some household items for a move, and I was unsure how much paper to use.

My friend placed the bowl in the middle of a sheet and folded the corners over it one at a time. What caught my attention was that she supported the inside of the bowl with loosely folded paper before wrapping the outside. She also left a soft layer underneath it in the box instead of putting it directly on the bottom.

I then wrapped a similar bowl while she watched. I initially pulled the paper too tightly, and she suggested leaving a little space so it would act as a cushion. Seeing her movements made that advice much clearer than the words alone would have done.

The observation helped me understand the purpose of each step. I was not simply copying a shape: I was trying to stop the bowl from moving and protect it from pressure. I would still ask for specialist advice before packing anything unusually valuable, but the method was useful for our ordinary dishes.""",
 fullp1=['Do you like taking photographs?','What do you usually photograph?','Do you keep old photographs?','How do you organise pictures?','Do you prefer printed pictures or digital ones?','Do you ever show pictures to friends?'],
 fullcard='Describe a photograph that helped you remember a place.\n\nSay:\n- what place it showed\n- when you saw the photograph\n- what details you noticed\n- and explain why it helped you remember.',
 fullround=['Would you like to return to that place?','Would someone else understand its importance?'],
 fulllong="""A photograph that helped me remember a place showed a small courtyard beside a guesthouse. I had stayed there during a short trip with my cousin, but I had almost forgotten the arrangement of the buildings until I found the picture while sorting some files.

The photograph was not especially impressive. It showed a wooden table, two blue chairs and a narrow doorway at the back. Yet the chairs reminded me that we had eaten breakfast outside because the kitchen was being cleaned. I also noticed a pot of herbs beside the doorway, which brought back the smell of the meal our host prepared that morning.

The picture helped me place several memories in the same setting. Before seeing it, I remembered a conversation with my cousin but could not clearly picture where we had been sitting. The angle of the table made that connection easier.

Of course, a photograph cannot preserve everything about a visit. It did not tell me the exact date or the words we used. Still, those small visible details gave me a useful starting point for recalling the atmosphere of the place.""",
 p3=['Why do people keep records of their experiences?','Are photographs always reliable records?','How can museums explain the limits of historical evidence?','Does keeping more information necessarily improve understanding?'],
 deepfollow=['What other evidence could support a photograph?','Could selecting only attractive pictures change a story?'],
 deep="A photograph can provide useful evidence of what was visible at one moment, but it cannot explain everything outside the frame. A crowded street, for instance, might reflect an unusual event rather than normal conditions. To understand it, we may need a date, a description of the occasion and other records. I think the important distinction is between what we can actually see and the explanation we add to it."),
 dict(w=23,focus='根据真实录音查缺补漏；提出一个可核查的真人反馈问题',
 p1=['Do you like discussing ideas with other people?','Whom do you ask when you need an explanation?','Do you prefer advice in person or in writing?','How do you decide whether advice is useful?'],
 p1follow=['What if two people give different advice?','Can advice be useful even if you do not follow it?'],
 short="I find advice useful when the person explains the reason behind it. If someone simply tells me to choose a different method, I may not understand what will improve. A small example helps me compare the suggestion with what I already do, and then I can decide whether to try it.",
 card='Describe a conversation that helped you understand a task.\n\nSay:\n- what the task was\n- whom you spoke to\n- what you discussed\n- and explain what became clearer.',
 round=['Did you need any further information?','Could the explanation have been given another way?'],
 long="""A conversation that helped me understand a task took place before a small community book sale. I had volunteered to arrange the books, but the message I received only said that they should be sorted into sections.

I spoke to the organiser because I was unsure whether those sections meant subjects, prices or the age of the readers. She explained that visitors would first choose between books for adults and books for children. Within those two areas, we could use broad subject labels. The price would be shown on a sticker on each book, so separate price tables were unnecessary.

I repeated the plan in my own words and asked where picture books with very little text should go. She pointed to the children's area and suggested keeping them near the front, where families could see them easily. We then drew a quick layout on a sheet of paper.

After that conversation, I could begin without guessing the purpose of the sections. The most useful part was not receiving more instructions in general; it was resolving the particular uncertainty that had stopped me from starting. I still checked unusual items with the organiser as we worked.""",
 fullp1=['Do you enjoy visiting libraries?','What makes a shared space comfortable?','Do you prefer working alone or with others?','What kinds of signs do you find useful?','How do you react when a place changes?','Would you like to help improve a local facility?'],
 fullcard='Describe a change you noticed in a public place.\n\nSay:\n- what the place was\n- what changed\n- how you noticed it\n- and explain your opinion of the change.',
 fullround=['Did everyone seem to use the change in the same way?','What would you want to know before judging its success?'],
 fulllong="""I noticed a change in the entrance area of a library near my home. Previously, a large noticeboard stood just inside the door. On a later visit, I saw that the board had moved to a side wall and a low table with a simple floor plan had taken its place.

The difference caught my attention because I had once stopped in the doorway to read a notice and blocked someone carrying books. With the new arrangement, I could move to the side before reading. The floor plan also showed the stairs and the enquiry desk with large symbols, which I found easy to recognise.

My first impression was positive. I spent less time deciding where to go, and the entrance seemed easier to move through during my visit. However, I only saw it on one quiet afternoon, so I cannot say that it solved every problem for every visitor.

I would be interested to hear from library staff and people who use mobility aids before making a stronger judgement. For me, the change was a helpful reminder that a small adjustment to the position of information can affect how a place feels.""",
 p3=['How should public organisations collect feedback?','Why might different users judge the same change differently?','Should every suggestion be acted on?','How can an organisation explain a decision to users?'],
 deepfollow=['What if the people most affected rarely respond?','How can organisations tell whether a change helped?'],
 deep="Public organisations should collect feedback in more than one way because a single survey may miss people who have limited time or difficulty using it. Short conversations and accessible written forms can reveal different experiences. However, collecting opinions is only a first step. The organisation should connect a suggestion to a specific problem, consider who is affected and explain what evidence supports its decision. Popularity alone does not show that a change will work."),
 dict(w=24,focus='依据已知证据解释选择；承认信息缺口，避免无条件结论',
 p1=['How do you choose between different activities?','Do you make important decisions quickly?','What information do you look for before joining a course?','Do you ever change a decision after learning more?'],
 p1follow=['When is it reasonable to delay a decision?','Does having more choice always help?'],
 short="Before joining a course, I would look at what participants actually do and how much time it requires. A title can sound attractive without telling me whether it suits my needs. If the information is incomplete, I would ask for a sample schedule instead of assuming that every session follows the same format.",
 card='Describe a time you waited for more information before making a choice.\n\nSay:\n- what choice you faced\n- what information was missing\n- how you obtained it\n- and explain whether waiting helped.',
 round=['Could waiting have had a disadvantage?','Would you use the same approach again?'],
 long="""I once waited for more information before choosing a weekend art workshop. There were two options at a local centre, and both descriptions said that beginners were welcome. One focused on drawing objects, while the other involved working with clay.

I was interested in the clay workshop, but I could not tell whether the listed fee included materials or whether participants needed to return on another day to collect their work. Since I was only free that weekend, the second point mattered quite a lot. I sent the centre a short enquiry and kept the drawing workshop as an alternative.

The reply explained that the clay pieces would need to be fired later and collected the following week. A friend could have collected mine, but arranging that would have added another complication. I eventually chose the drawing session, where I could take my work home on the same day.

Waiting helped because the missing information was directly connected to my schedule. It did not prove that drawing was a better activity in general. It simply allowed me to choose the option that fitted my circumstances at that time.""",
 fullp1=['Do you enjoy setting personal goals?','Do you prefer short projects or longer ones?','How do you keep track of a project?','Do you celebrate finishing something?','What do you do when a plan takes longer than expected?','Who do you talk to about future plans?'],
 fullcard='Describe a project whose next step you had to decide.\n\nSay:\n- what the project was\n- what had already been done\n- what options you considered\n- and explain how you decided what to do next.',
 fullround=['Could the decision change later?','What information would have made the choice easier?'],
 fulllong="""A project whose next step I had to decide was a small collection of family recipes. I had typed several handwritten recipes into a document so that relatives could read them more easily. Once the first version was ready, I considered printing a booklet or asking everyone to check it before doing anything else.

The document looked complete, but two recipes used expressions such as a small cup without explaining the size. I also did not know whether I had copied one cooking temperature correctly. Printing immediately would have been satisfying, yet it might have made those uncertain details harder to correct later.

I decided to send the draft to the relatives who had provided the recipes and ask specific questions about those points. I marked the uncertain measurements clearly, so nobody would mistake them for confirmed instructions. While waiting, I worked on the contents page, which did not depend on their answers.

That choice let the project continue without pretending that every part was finished. If the replies confirmed the details, printing could become the next step. If they raised further questions, I would revise the draft first. The decision depended on the information available, rather than simply on how long I had already spent.""",
 p3=['What evidence should people consider before starting a major project?','Why do people sometimes continue with an unsuitable plan?','When is extending a deadline reasonable?','How can people compare progress with their original goals?'],
 deepfollow=['Can an uncertain result still be useful?','What if different measures of progress disagree?'],
 deep="An uncertain result can still be useful if it shows exactly what remains unknown. For example, a trial might reveal that a method is practical without showing whether it works reliably over time. That supports a further check, rather than an immediate claim of success or failure. When measures disagree, I would examine whether they assess the same goal and whether the conditions were comparable before making a decision.")
]
